%requires qore >= 3.0

%modern

%disable-warning unreferenced-variable

%requires json
%requires Mime
%requires Util
%requires astparser
%requires QoreCodeCompletion
%requires QoreCodeFormat
%requires QoreApiMetadata

module QLS {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "Qore Language Server (LSP) implementation";
    author = "Qore Technologies, s.r.o.";
    url = "https://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage QLS Module

    @section qlsintro QLS Module Introduction

    The %QLS module provides a full-featured
    <a href="https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/">Language Server Protocol</a>
    (LSP) implementation for %Qore, enabling code intelligence in any editor that supports
    the LSP standard. It uses the @c astparser binary module's tree-sitter CST for static
    analysis, delivering 25+ language features without requiring code execution.

    The module provides the following classes:
    - @ref QLS::QLSServer "QLSServer": main JSON-RPC server handling all LSP requests and
      notifications; call @ref QLS::QLSServer::main() "main()" to run the server loop
    - @ref QLS::Document "Document": document model wrapping the @c astparser CST; provides
      symbol search, diagnostics, completion, formatting, folding, and cross-document resolution
    - @ref QLS::BuiltinResolver "BuiltinResolver": resolves expressions to builtin functions, classes, namespaces,
      typed hashes and the methods of basic types (see @ref qls_builtins)
    - @ref QLS::FileIndex "FileIndex": compact symbol index of a source file that is not open in the editor
    - @ref QLS::DocumentCache "DocumentCache": size-limited cache of syntax trees parsed from indexed files
    - @ref QLS::Messenger "Messenger": JSON-RPC transport layer reading/writing LSP messages
      on stdin/stdout with @c Content-Length headers
    - @ref QLS::LspConnection "LspConnection": LSP message connection on standard input and output or on any
      pair of streams
    - @ref QLS::DocumentLinks "DocumentLinks": finds the files of module and include directives
    - @ref QLS::TextDiff "TextDiff": line differences and minimal text edits between two texts
    - @ref QLS::ErrorResponse "ErrorResponse": factory for LSP error responses
      (@c InvalidRequest, @c MethodNotFound, @c InternalError, etc.)
    - @ref QLS::Notification "Notification": factory for LSP notifications
      (@c textDocument/publishDiagnostics, @c window/showMessage, etc.)
    - @ref QLS::ParamValidator "ParamValidator": validates incoming request parameters against
      required fields and types

    @section qlsfeatures Supported LSP Features

    The following table lists all LSP features supported by QLS, organized by category:

    <b>Navigation</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Hover|@c textDocument/hover|Shows type, documentation, and source info for the symbol under the cursor; declarations in the source show the documentation of their @c #! and @c /** doc comments, with parameters, return values, exceptions and code examples, also while the document has syntax errors, as do signature help and completion items; members accessed with @c . are resolved by the types of their receivers, including the methods of basic types and the members of typed hashes (see @ref qls_builtins)
    |Go to Definition|@c textDocument/definition|Jumps to the definition of a symbol
    |Go to Declaration|@c textDocument/declaration|Jumps to the declaration of a symbol; declarations are definitions in %Qore
    |Document Links|@c textDocument/documentLink|Links the module names and paths of @c %%requires, @c %%try-module and @c %%include directives to their source files, resolving module names like %Qore with the document's module path directives, @c QORE_MODULE_DIR and the library module path
    |Go to Type Definition|@c textDocument/typeDefinition|Jumps to the type definition of a symbol
    |Go to Implementation|@c textDocument/implementation|Jumps to the implementation of an abstract method or interface
    |Find References|@c textDocument/references|Finds all references to a symbol across open and workspace documents

    <b>Code Intelligence</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Code Completion|@c textDocument/completion|Context-aware completions with trigger characters @c . (member access), @c : (namespace/static), and normal identifier completion; uses @ref qorecodecompletionintro "QoreCodeCompletion"; callables are inserted as snippets with the cursor between the parentheses, VS Code clients are asked to show parameter hints and further suggestions after callables and namespaces, and documentation is Markdown when the client supports it; the members of builtin classes include inherited members, and the receivers of @c . can be calls such as @c now() (see @ref qls_builtins)
    |Signature Help|@c textDocument/signatureHelp|Function/method parameter hints with trigger characters @c ( and @c ,, including the functions of builtin namespaces and the methods of builtin types
    |Inlay Hints|@c textDocument/inlayHint|Shows parameter names at call sites as inline hints
    |Semantic Tokens|@c textDocument/semanticTokens/full, @c .../full/delta and @c .../range|Full semantic syntax highlighting with node-level classification
    |Document Highlight|@c textDocument/documentHighlight|Highlights all occurrences of the symbol under the cursor
    |Linked Editing|@c textDocument/linkedEditingRange|Edits the occurrences of a local variable or parameter together; occurrences of variables with the same name in other functions are not included
    |Inline Values|@c textDocument/inlineValue|Shows the values of the local variables and parameters of the function where the debugger stopped, in the visible lines up to the stopped line

    <b>Refactoring</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Rename|@c textDocument/rename (with @c textDocument/prepareRename)|Renames a symbol across all documents in the workspace
    |File Rename|@c workspace/willRenameFiles|Updates the @c %%include and module path directives that refer to renamed or moved %Qore files, and the relative paths of moved files; relative, @c ${SCRIPT_DIR} and absolute paths keep their form (paths use @c / as the separator)
    |Code Actions|@c textDocument/codeAction|Quick fixes: sort imports, add @c %modern directive

    <b>Code Structure</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Document Symbols|@c textDocument/documentSymbol|Hierarchical outline of all symbols in a document
    |Workspace Symbols|@c workspace/symbol, @c workspaceSymbol/resolve|Search for symbols across all workspace files; clients that support resolving the ranges of workspace symbols get locations without ranges, which are resolved when a symbol is selected
    |Folding Ranges|@c textDocument/foldingRange|Code folding for declarations, blocks, closures, multi-line hash and list literals, and multi-line comments
    |Selection Range|@c textDocument/selectionRange|Expanding selection based on syntactic structure

    <b>Hierarchy</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Type Hierarchy|@c textDocument/prepareTypeHierarchy, @c typeHierarchy/supertypes, @c typeHierarchy/subtypes|Navigate class inheritance (supertypes and subtypes)
    |Call Hierarchy|@c textDocument/prepareCallHierarchy, @c callHierarchy/incomingCalls, @c callHierarchy/outgoingCalls|Navigate call graphs (incoming and outgoing calls)

    <b>Formatting and Display</b>
    |!Feature|!LSP Method|!Description
    |Document Formatting|@c textDocument/formatting|Whole-document formatting via @ref qorecodeformatintro "QoreCodeFormat"
    |Range Formatting|@c textDocument/rangeFormatting|Formats the selected lines: the whole document is formatted, as the formatter needs the complete syntax tree, and only the changed lines that overlap the selection are returned
    |On-Type Formatting|@c textDocument/onTypeFormatting|Auto-indent on Enter, auto-dedent on @c }
    |Code Lens|@c textDocument/codeLens|Shows reference counts across open and workspace documents above declarations; the lenses run the \c qls.showReferences client command with the document URI and the position of the declared name
    |Diagnostics|@c textDocument/publishDiagnostics, @c textDocument/diagnostic, @c workspace/diagnostic|Reports Qore parse errors and warnings for standalone scripts and syntax errors for other files (see @ref qls_diagnostics)

    @section qlsusage Usage

    @subsection qls_editor Editor Integration

    QLS is installed as the @c qls system command and communicates via JSON-RPC over
    stdin/stdout, making it compatible with any editor that supports the LSP standard.

    @subsection qls_vscode VS Code Configuration

    Add the following to your VS Code @c settings.json to use QLS:

    @code{.json}
{
    "qore.executable": "/usr/bin/qore",
    "qore.useQLS": true
}
    @endcode

    @subsection qls_programmatic Programmatic Usage

    @code{.py}
%requires QLS

# Create and run the server (reads stdin, writes stdout)
QLS::QLSServer server();
server.main();
    @endcode

    For testing, you can call @ref QLS::QLSServer::handleRequest() "handleRequest()" directly
    with JSON-RPC message strings to simulate editor requests.

    @section qlsarchitecture Architecture

    @verbatim
    Editor  <-->  JSON-RPC (Messenger)  <-->  QLSServer  <-->  Document  <-->  astparser/CSTSearcher
    @endverbatim

    The @ref QLS::QLSServer "QLSServer" maintains three document stores:
    - <b>Open documents</b> (@c documents): files currently open in the editor, updated on
      every @c textDocument/didChange notification; open documents keep their syntax trees
    - <b>Workspace index</b> (@c workspaceIndex): Qore and Qorus source files with extensions
      listed in @ref QLS::Files::QORE_EXTENSIONS, discovered in all workspace folders and indexed on
      initialization; folders added to or removed from the workspace
      (@c workspace/didChangeWorkspaceFolders) are indexed or removed
    - <b>Standard module index</b> (@c stdModuleIndex): %Qore standard library modules, indexed from
      the module path when a request first needs them; when the modules are loaded from compiled qmods, their
      sources are found in the source tree of an in-tree build or in the user module directory of an installed
      %Qore (see @ref QLS::Files::get_std_module_dirs() "get_std_module_dirs()")

    Cross-document features (find references, rename, call hierarchy) search across all three
    stores to provide workspace-wide results.

    @subsection qls_indexing Workspace Indexing

    A syntax tree takes roughly 20 times the size of its source in memory, so files that are not
    open in the editor are not kept as syntax trees.  Each file is parsed once, the symbols that it
    declares are stored in a @ref QLS::FileIndex "FileIndex", and the syntax tree is released.
    Symbol searches (workspace symbols, hover, definition and type lookups) use the indexes
    directly.

    When a request needs the syntax tree of an indexed file, only the files that can contain the
    result are parsed:
    - references, rename, code lenses and incoming calls: the files whose source contains the identifier;
      each index has a filter of the identifiers in the file
      (@ref astparser::AstTree::getIdentifierFilter() "AstTree::getIdentifierFilter()"), so most files are
      excluded without being read
    - definitions: the files that declare classes on the inheritance path from a class used in
      the document to a class with a member of that name, and the files that declare the
      identifier, in batches and in the original search order
    - hover, subtypes and implementations: the files with matching symbols or subclasses

    Parsed trees are kept in a @ref QLS::DocumentCache "DocumentCache" limited by the total size of
    their sources.  @ref QLS::QLSServer::getIndexInfo() "QLSServer::getIndexInfo()" returns the
    current size of the indexes and the cache.

    Workspace scanning skips subdirectories matching @ref QLS::Files::DefaultExcludeDirs (version
    control metadata, dependency trees, agent worktrees and build output), files larger than
    @ref QLS::Files::DefaultMaxIndexFileSize, and directories already reached through another path,
    so symbolic links cannot cause loops or duplicate results.

    These limits can be set with the @c initializationOptions of the @c initialize request:
    |!Option|!Type|!Description
    |@c excludeDirs|list of strings|shell-style glob patterns (@c * and @c ?) for subdirectory names that are not searched; replaces @ref QLS::Files::DefaultExcludeDirs
    |@c maxIndexFileSize|int|files larger than this many bytes are not indexed; @c 0 means no limit (default: @ref QLS::Files::DefaultMaxIndexFileSize)
    |@c treeCacheSize|int|syntax trees of indexed files are cached up to this many bytes of source; @c 0 disables the cache (default: @ref QLS::DefaultTreeCacheSize)
    |@c diagnostics|string|@c "qore" (the default) to check standalone scripts with the %Qore parser, or @c "syntax" to only report syntax errors (see @ref qls_diagnostics)
    |@c qoreExecutable|string|the %Qore executable used to check scripts; by default the interpreter running the server

    For example:
    @code{.json}
{
    "initializationOptions": {
        "excludeDirs": [".git", "node_modules", "build*", "generated"],
        "maxIndexFileSize": 4194304,
        "treeCacheSize": 16777216
    }
}
    @endcode

    Options with invalid values are logged and ignored.

    @subsection qls_loop Server Loop

    @ref QLS::QLSServer::main() "QLSServer::main()" reads messages in a background thread, so that
    @c $/cancelRequest notifications are seen while a request is processed.  The workspace is indexed after the
    client's @c initialized notification, one file at a time while no message is waiting, so requests are answered
    during indexing with the files indexed so far:
    - clients that support work done progress (@c window.workDoneProgress) are asked to create a progress token
      and get @c begin, @c report and @c end notifications for the workspace files
    - standard module files are indexed after the workspace files, so the first lookup in a standard module does
      not have to wait for them
    - a cancelled request is answered with the @c RequestCancelled error, before it is started or at the next file
      that it would search
    - errors in request handlers are answered with the request ID, invalid JSON with a @c ParseError response, and
      responses from the client to requests sent by the server are not answered

    Applications that call @ref QLS::QLSServer::handleRequest() "handleRequest()" themselves can use the same
    behavior: enable it with @ref QLS::QLSServer::setBackgroundIndexing() "setBackgroundIndexing()", call
    @ref QLS::QLSServer::indexPendingFile() "indexPendingFile()" while no message is waiting, send the messages
    returned by @ref QLS::QLSServer::takeQueuedMessages() "takeQueuedMessages()", and report request IDs with
    @ref QLS::QLSServer::registerRequest() "registerRequest()" and
    @ref QLS::QLSServer::cancelRequest() "cancelRequest()".  To run the server on other streams, pass an
    @ref QLS::LspConnection "LspConnection" to @ref QLS::QLSServer::main() "main()".

    @subsection qls_diagnostics Diagnostics

    Standalone scripts (@c .q, @c .qr and @c .qtest files, and files without an extension that start with a
    @c qore @c "#!" line) are checked with the %Qore parser when they are opened and when they are saved:
    <tt>qore --diag-format=json -W</tt> parses the file in a separate process, so the modules that the script
    requires are loaded there and not in the server.  The diagnostics include type errors, unresolved symbols,
    errors in required modules (at their @c %%requires directive) and warnings, and are shown while the document has
    not changed since it was checked; after a change, the syntax errors of the syntax tree are shown until the
    document is saved again.  A script is only checked when the editor text is the same as the file.

    Module, class and Qorus files are parts of a larger program, so only their syntax errors are reported.

    The fixes of %Qore diagnostics are offered as @c quickfix code actions; machine-applicable fixes are
    preferred.

    Clients that support pull diagnostics and diagnostic refresh requests pull the same diagnostics with
    @c textDocument/diagnostic and are asked to pull them again after a document is saved and checked; other
    clients get them with @c textDocument/publishDiagnostics notifications.

    These clients also pull the diagnostics of the workspace with @c workspace/diagnostic: those of the open
    documents, and the syntax errors of the other workspace files, which are found when the files are indexed, so
    files with syntax errors are reported without opening them.  Files that are not open are not checked with the
    %Qore parser, as that would run a process for every script of the workspace.  Files whose diagnostics have not
    changed since the client's last result are reported as unchanged, and clients are asked to pull the
    diagnostics again when background indexing is done and when workspace files change.

    @subsection qls_sync Document Synchronization and Symbols

    Open documents are synchronized incrementally (@c TextDocumentSyncKind.Incremental): clients send only the
    changed ranges, with positions in UTF-16 code units as defined by LSP.  Save notifications are requested.

    @c textDocument/documentSymbol returns a tree of @c DocumentSymbol objects when the client supports
    hierarchical document symbols: symbols are nested in their namespaces and classes, named without prefixes,
    and their @c selectionRange is the declared name; local variables of functions and methods are not included.
    Clients without this capability, and requests with the @c retType parameter used by the VS Code extension
    API, get a flat list of @c SymbolInformation objects with qualified names.

    @subsection qls_builtins Builtin Symbols

    Hover, completion and signature help cover the builtin API of %Qore: its functions, classes, constants,
    hashdecls, enums and namespaces, which are extracted with the @c reflection module and documented with the
    qpp metadata installed with %Qore, and the pseudo-classes of the basic types, which are only in the qpp
    metadata: the methods of @c \<string\> can be called on strings, for example, and the methods of @c \<value\> on
    all values.

    Members accessed with @c . are resolved by the types of their receivers, which
    @ref astparser::AstTreeSearcher::getExpressionChain() returns as chains of variables, calls and members; in
    @code{.py}
string text = now().format("YYYY-MM-DD") + ": " + hstat(path).size;
    @endcode
    @c format is the method of dates, as @c now() returns a date, and @c size is the member of the
    @c StatInfo hashdecl that @c hstat() returns.  The members of a builtin class include the members of its
    parent classes and the methods of the @c \<object\> and @c \<value\> pseudo-classes, and typed hashes have the
    methods of the @c \<hash\> pseudo-class.  Names declared in the open documents or the workspace, such as a
    function named like a builtin function, are resolved as source symbols instead of builtin symbols.

    @section qlsrelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection qls_v1_0 v1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! Main namespace for the QLS module
public namespace QLS {
}
